.TH FESCAPE 1 "September 2026"
.SH NAME
fescape \- escape non-printable bytes in a stream
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fescape
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.RI [ FILE " ..."]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Copy printable ASCII bytes (0x20 through 0x7e), replacing other bytes with
hexadecimal escapes such as <0x00>. Newline bytes are preserved by default.
Input is interpreted as bytes, not Unicode characters.
With no files, read standard input. A file named \- reads standard input at
that position. Files are processed in order with no added separators.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Display help and exit.
.TP
.B \-r, \-\-repeats
Collapse runs of escaped bytes into <code>[count], including the first byte
in the count. Counts reset for each input. Unescaped newlines are preserved.
.TP
.B \-n, \-\-newline
Escape newlines as <0x0a> (or <012> with \-o).
.TP
.B \-o, \-\-octal
Use three-digit octal escapes instead of hexadecimal.
.TP
.B \-\-
End option parsing; useful for filenames beginning with a dash.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
fescape binary.dat
fescape \-rn file1 file2
fescape file1 \- file2
fescape \-\- \-filename
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero on success, nonzero for invalid options or input/output failures.
Input failures are reported on standard error; subsequent files are processed.
.SH NOTES
Place options before filenames. Output is independent of the locale.
.SH AUTHOR
Robert Primmer
